Orion O6 fix for DP audio turning robotic, then silent. Same approach as CIX's own
mainline series "dmaengine: arm-dma350: Add slave support and Sky1 integration"
(v7, Nov 2026, patch 08/10 "Add slave and cyclic transfer support", not merged yet),
whose cyclic residue code says: "Locate the current period from the next command's
address rather than counting interrupts, since multiple period completions may share a
pending DONE interrupt."

The vendor DMA-350 driver works out the position of cyclic (audio) transfers only by
counting "period done" interrupts (desnum[]). The DONE status is a single bit, so if
the interrupt is held off for more than one period (256 frames = 5.3 ms at 48 kHz),
two completions are seen as one and the position reported to ALSA stays one period
behind the hardware for good. Every further miss adds another period, until the app
writes into the part of the ring the DMA has already played: first repeated
fragments ("robotic"), then stale data or silence. On this board long interrupt-off
stretches came from kernel messages written to the 115200-baud serial console (0014).

Fix:
- The position reported to ALSA (tx_status) is read from the hardware: CH_LINKADDR
  (address of the command after the executing one -> current period) and the
  destination part of CH_XSIZE/CH_XSIZEHI (what is left of that period), with retries
  if the channel moves on between the reads. The DMA-side address of every period's
  command is recorded when the command chain is built.
- The interrupt count stays as a fallback; on every period interrupt it is
  resynchronised from the hardware (read before the interrupt status is cleared).
  Resynchronised misses are counted in /sys/module/arm_dma350/parameters/lost_periods,
  with a one-time warning in dmesg.
- Two per-stream-start messages ("use dynamic channel-N", "Channel N initialized with
  N periods") are now debug level.

